#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The groups, loaded OVER THE WIRE rather than with slapadd.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 🛑 And that is the whole reason this is a separate file.
#
# The memberof overlay maintains `memberOf` on a user entry as a group entry
# is written -- it reacts to writes it SEES. `slapadd` loads a database
# offline, with no server running and no overlay in the path, so groups
# seeded that way leave every user with no `memberOf` at all. Measured: the
# directory answered a search for dana with `uid` and `cn` and nothing else,
# and the group test failed over an empty set in both directions -- which is
# exactly the shape of defect this phase keeps finding.
#
# So the entrypoint starts slapd on its local socket, adds these entries
# through it, stops it, and only then starts the real server.
# 🛑 `groupOfNames` with `member` DNs, which is the only direction a plain
# directory stores membership — and the entrypoint loads the `memberof`
# overlay so the same fact also appears as `memberOf` on the USER entry,
# which is the direction the driver reads. The note beside that overlay says
# why it is load-bearing rather than convenient.
#
# So removing a member here is the group removal §17's exit criterion means,
# and it is one `ldapmodify` with `delete: member`.
dn: cn=docs-readers,ou=groups,dc=docup,dc=test
objectClass: groupOfNames
cn: docs-readers
member: uid=dana,ou=people,dc=docup,dc=test
#
# 🛑 A SECOND MEMBER, and it is not padding.
#
# `groupOfNames` makes `member` a MUST attribute, so a group with no members
# is a schema violation and `ldap_mod_del` of the last one is refused with
# "Object class violation (65)". Measured: the exit criterion's removal
# failed on it, and the symptom named the object class rather than the
# cause.
#
# So dana is not the only member. The service account is used rather than
# morgan, deliberately: morgan is the reader who must hold NO group, and
# putting them here would make `a reader in no group holds no group` pass or
# fail depending on which test ran first.
#
# This is also how a real directory looks -- a group with exactly one member
# is the unusual case -- so the fixture is more faithful for it, not less.
member: cn=svc-docup,dc=docup,dc=test

dn: cn=docs-admins,ou=groups,dc=docup,dc=test
objectClass: groupOfNames
cn: docs-admins
member: cn=svc-docup,dc=docup,dc=test
